Subject: [PATCH 0/5] drm/lima: fix a heap-grow page fault and the recovery from MMU faults
To: Qiang Yu <yuq825@gmail.com>
Cc: Erico Nunes <nunes.erico@gmail.com>,
    Danilo Krummrich <dakr@kernel.org>,
    Maarten Lankhorst <maarten.lankhorst@linux.intel.com>,
    Maxime Ripard <mripard@kernel.org>,
    Thomas Zimmermann <tzimmermann@suse.de>,
    David Airlie <airlied@gmail.com>,
    Simona Vetter <simona@ffwll.ch>,
    lima@lists.freedesktop.org,
    dri-devel@lists.freedesktop.org,
    linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org

On an Allwinner A10 (Mali-400 MP1) desktop, short glmark2 runs wedge the
GPU every few runs until a reboot:

  lima 1c40000.gpu: gpmmu page fault at 0x45c9000 from bus id 0 of type read
  lima 1c40000.gpu: gp task error int_state=0 status=a
  lima 1c40000.gpu: gp bus stop timeout

followed by a GP job timeout and the same bus stop timeout every 10 s.
Two separate problems meet here.

The trigger (patch 5): when the PLBU runs out of tile heap,
lima_sched_recover_work() flushes the L2 cache and zaps the TLB before
lima_gp_task_recover() maps the new heap pages, so the restarted PLBU can
use a stale "not present" page table entry. The fault always hits the
first page of the 32 -> 64 KiB grow of the tile heap of the second GP job
of a new GL context, and the PTE is valid in memory at fault time. Mesa
could avoid it by starting with a larger heap, but any heap grow can
hit it, so the kernel has to flush in the right order.

The recovery (patches 2-4): an MMU fault on a heap job is handled as
out-of-heap (patch 2), the core is hard-reset while the MMU still holds
the faulting transaction, so the bus never stops and the GPU stays dead
(patch 3), and a queued heap recovery can run after the reset (patch 4).
Patch 1 fixes an oops in the error task dump (lima.max_error_tasks > 0),
which I hit while debugging this.

Evidence per patch: 1 oops seen and gone; 2 and 3 the fault recovered
instead of wedging the GPU (one fault seen with them); 4 found by review,
not seen on hardware (no Cc: stable for it); 5 an A/B test, see below.

The patches are independent of each other and apply in any order.
Older stable trees will need context fixes, and patch 1 uses the
two-argument strscpy() (v6.8+). Patch 3 is the largest: it adds a
three-page "fault flush" page directory per device.

Patches 3 and 5 refer to ARM's Mali-400 kernel driver (r3p0) for the
order of operations only; the code is written for lima from scratch.

Testing, all on Armbian 6.18.56 (its lima differs from drm-misc-next
only in allocation helpers and includes), Mesa 25.0.7, Xorg, a loop of
glmark2-es2 -b build:use-vbo=true -b shading:shading=gouraud
-b bump:bump-render=high-poly:

- Allwinner A10 (Mali-400 MP1, Rikomagic MK802):
  - unpatched: the GPU wedged within about 10 runs;
  - patches 1-4: the fault still occurs, and lima now recovers within
    1 ms (the dependent PP job fails, rendering continues);
  - with lima.heap_init_nr_pages=1 (many heap grows per frame): patches
    1-4 faulted in 5 of 20 runs, patches 1-5 in 0 of 40;
  - patches 1-5 with the default heap: 0 faults in 80 runs.
- Allwinner A20 (Mali-400 MP2, Cubietruck): patches 1-5 run on the
  desktop since 2026-11-01; this board had one fault at 0x9000 (tile
  heap at VA 0x1000, the same grow step) followed by the same dead GPU
  before. That fault is rare here, so this is no proof for the A20.
- The version of patch 4 that ran on these boards cancelled the work
  without the second irq mask; this one adds it after review.
- Not tested: Mali-450 (patch 3 handles the broadcast MMU like
  lima_mmu_page_fault_resume() does, but that path has not run), v7.x
  kernels on hardware. The series builds with W=1 for ARM
  (sunxi_defconfig) after each patch and for arm64 (defconfig).

Left as they are: lima_heap_alloc() remaps the whole heap on every
grow, the L2 flush result is not checked, and the heap recovery
allocates memory in the fence signalling path.

The patches and this letter were written with an AI assistant; I
reviewed and tested them.

D.R.racer (5):
  drm/lima: do not use the scheduler entity when saving an error task
  drm/lima: do not handle a GP MMU fault as an out-of-heap condition
  drm/lima: let a faulted core finish its bus transactions before the
    reset
  drm/lima: cancel a pending heap recovery in the timeout handler
  drm/lima: flush the L2 cache and TLB after growing the heap

